# WORKFLOW — Write Map, Execution Cycle, Multi-Agent Protocols & Historical Defects
File: openOODA/practices/ooda-workflow.oot

This document defines target file locations, execution cycles, multi-agent protocols, and defect lessons.
Read Section 0 before modifying files in any directory.

## 0. Product Write Map — Target Modification Locations

Three public components form the external interface:
- Web Portal: `openOODA/website/index.html` (https://openooda.org/).
- Documentation: `openOODA/website/guide/*.oot` (https://openooda.org/guide/).
- Package Store: (https://openooda.org/#registry).

### 0.1 Modification Targets by Subsystem

- Package Manager & CLI Commands:
  Target: `ooda/ooda/cmd_pm*.oo`, `ooda/ooda/pm_*.oo`, `ooda/ooda/main.oo`.
  Verification: `ooda/ooda/*_proof.oo` and `qa/pm_*_spine.oo`.
  Do not grow `ooda/pm/`.

- Compiler & Language Passes:
  Target: `ooda/oodac/*.oo` and `ooda/scripts/*_smoke.oo`.
  Rebuild Script: `ooda/bootstrap/oodac_pure_build`.
  Never copy `./bin/ooda` onto `./oodac/oodac`.

- Runtime C Components:
  Target: `openOODA/oodar/`. Restricted to runtime engineer roles. The runtime has been an independent polyrepo (oodar/) since the v0.1.0 extraction; the v1.0.0/v1.0.1 6-subdir reorg and the v2.0.0 Floor break live there. The `ooda/runtime/` path is the legacy location and is no longer the build entry point.

- Standard Library:
  Target: `ooda/std/`. Ships with the compiler. Verify with emission, compilation, and execution tests.

- Public Documentation & Guides:
  Target: `openOODA/website/guide/*.oot`.

- Formal RFC Proposals:
  Target: `openOODA/rfcs/0000-name.oot`. Copy `rfcs/0000-template.oot`.

- Public Installer:
  Target: `ooda/ooda/cmd_install.oo` (Gen 2 compiler install) and host script `openOODA/website/install.sh`.

- Canonical Governance Process Boards:
  Target: The 13 canonical process boards in `openOODA/` + `LLMS.oot` orientation codex. Update `SHIPPED.oot` only via `scripts/shipped_generate.oo`.

### 0.2 Prohibited Write Locations
- Do not write to local `wiki/` or `docs/` directories.
- Keep secret cryptographic keys off Git repositories (`~/.ooda-keys/`).

### 0.3 Web Publishing Rules
- Use a monochrome user interface with monospace typography.
- Public web links remain on `openooda.org`.
- Push changes to `openOODA/website/` to publish through GitHub Pages.

## 1. OODA Execution Cycle & Turn Lifecycle

The OODA rotation guarantees continuous progress and clean git history on every turn:

0. Phase 0 (Adversarial Falsification Probe):
   - Author a failing test probing invalid bounds or edge cases before implementing code.
   - Execute the test to prove it fails closed on existing code.
   - Implement the multi-file architecture when complexity requires modular decomposition.
   - Run sequential double-run verification and inspect git diffs line-by-line for true algorithmic computation.

1. Observe:
   - Read Section 0 to identify target write directories.
   - Read active handoffs and release state (`v2.11.0`) in `ROADMAP.oot`.
   - Review historical defect lessons in Section 5.
   - Inspect line counts (<= 256 lines) and test status.
   - Check live git status and re-read target files from disk.

2. Decide (Select at Most 5 Items):
   Prioritize tasks in strict order:
   1. Fix broken tests and compilation errors.
   2. Fix security vulnerabilities and fail-closed defects.
   3. Implement next scheduled roadmap feature.
   4. Resolve architectural gaps.
   5. Clean and refactor code.

3. Act:
   - Write all code comments and documentation in ASD-STE100.
   - Implement modifications directly on the real product path.
   - Maintain the 256-line limit per file. Split files along domain seams.
   - Author executable tests with behavioral assertions.

4. Lock:
   - Verify line limits, dead code removal, and clean test runs.
   - Execute product rails and pure `.oo` test suites.
   - Regenerate `SHIPPED.oot` using `scripts/shipped_generate.oo`.
   - Double-run tests in fresh processes to confirm determinism.

5. Ship:
   - Create atomic Git commits with descriptive messages.
   - Author 5-component handoff reports in ASD-STE100.

## 2. Multi-Agent Concurrent Swarm Protocol (Pillar 6)

All autonomous agents must follow the 4-part concurrent protocol:

1. Continuous Git & Disk Awareness:
   - Re-read target files from disk and check git status before editing.
   - Never rely on cached file contents across turns.

2. Cooperative Non-Clobbering Integration:
   - Coordinate task assignments across the team to prevent overlapping edits.
   - Make atomic, granular commits that preserve peer modifications.

3. Version-Tagged Checkpoints & Structured Handoffs:
   - Document changes in 5-component handoffs (Observation, Logic Chain, Caveats, Conclusion, Verification Method).
   - Tag milestones with Generational SemVer (`v<Floor>.<Thrust>.<Patch>`, verified at `v2.11.0`).

4. The 8-Gate Blocking Veto Protocol & 8D Red Team:
   - Gate 1: Lowering AST integrity without keyword/string heuristics (Red 1 / e1).
   - Gate 2: Boundary limits and dynamic parameter ranges (Red 2 / e2).
   - Gate 3: Genuine execution wiring without stubs or fallbacks (Red 3 / e3).
   - Gate 4: Sandboxing and zero ambient authority isolation (Red 4 / e4).
   - Gate 5: Concurrency stream synchronization and replay determinism (Red 5 / e5).
   - Gate 6: Algorithmic complexity and zero memory leaks (Red 6 / e6).
   - Gate 7: Semantic mutation kill rate >= 95% and valid RFC templates (Red 7 / e7).
   - Gate 8: Constant-time cryptography (|t_Welch| < 4.5) and RASP rollback (Red 8 / e8).
   - Master Governance Gate: Run `openOODA/scripts/governance_enforce.oo` with zero violations.

## 3. Adversarial Validation & Negative Trust (Pillar 3)

Green tests are hypotheses rather than conclusive proofs. Adversarial testing actively attacks assumptions.

The Sequential Double-Run Law:
Execute `./bin/ooda run <test.oo>` twice in fresh processes. Both runs must pass identically to prove deterministic safety.

Hostile Probing Vectors:
1. Missing, forged, or ungranted capability tokens fail closed with explicit errors.
2. Resource limits: verify process behavior under file descriptor and memory limits.
3. Cleanup: ensure file descriptors and child processes terminate cleanly.
4. Test real distributed binaries directly rather than mocking internal subsystems.

## 4. Context Token Economy & Sparing Ingress

Context tokens are finite. Verbose text consumes reasoning budget.
Use `./bin/ooda outline <file.oo>` and `./bin/ooda reflect <file.oo> [sym]` to inspect code efficiently.
`./bin/ooda outline <file.oo>` consumes approximately 1.3% of raw source volume.
Never load raw emitted C dumps (`all*.c`), full `ROADMAP.oot`, or large GCC logs into context.

## 5. Historical Defect Lessons & Engineering Guidelines

1. AST Macro Injection: Always inject `((void)0)` instead of unbraced control constructs.
2. Struct Field Layouts: The compiler shuffles internal struct layouts; do not assume fixed field offsets.
3. Process Policy: Invoke `oo_process_policy_getenv` to respect capability security policies.
4. Capability Gating: New standard library primitives must fail closed until verified.
5. AST Import Nodes: Represent imports as distinct `IMPORT` AST nodes to maintain accurate item indexes.
6. Script File Law: Write all smoke tests as pure `.oo` files in `ooda/scripts/`.
7. FFI Handle Synchronization: Host dynamic library operations require `&UnsafeFFICap` and mutex locking.
8. CLI String Operations: Do not use `str_index_of(...) == 0` or `!= 0`. Writes require `OODA_FS_WRITEDIR`.
9. Binary Identity Invariant: Never copy the CLI binary onto the compiler binary.
10. Interface Stubs vs Wired Pipelines: Never declare public interfaces or types without wiring the full execution pipeline to real AST traversal and backend emission.

